1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,500
I pulled a property registration document.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,500
My name was not on it.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The home I had lived in for thirty years...

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,000
My name was not on it.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
I couldn't remember who had pressed that seal.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,000
I simply couldn't remember.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Today, for the very first time, I want to tell that story.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Suwon, Gyeonggi Province.

9
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,000
Unit 302, Maetan-dong Villa.

10
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:47,000
That was Kim Soonja's home.

11
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,000
A home she and her husband had bought together thirty years ago.

12
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:58,000
Even after her husband passed away first,
Soonja never left that place.

13
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:04,000
It was a small two-bedroom villa,
but her husband's traces remained in every corner.

14
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:08,000
A razor on the bathroom shelf.

15
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:12,500
A wind chime hanging by the living room window.

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Her husband had hung it himself —
she hadn't taken it down in fifteen years.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
[Mother]
"People ask if I get lonely after my husband died.
But somehow, I never felt lonely.

18
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:35,000
It felt like the house held me together.
When I was in that house... it felt like he was still beside me."

19
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Soonja was seventy years old.

20
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:43,000
She was still in good health.

21
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:49,000
Every morning she walked around the neighborhood.
Every Tuesday, a singing class at the welfare center.

22
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:55,000
Every Thursday, she stopped by her son's house to watch the grandchildren.

23
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:02,000
To everyone around her,
Soonja was having a fairly good old age.

24
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:08,000
What started to shake that —
it began with small things.

25
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:20,000
[Mother]
"At first I thought nothing of it.
I'd go to the kitchen wondering if I'd turned off the gas,
and then couldn't remember why I'd come.

26
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:30,000
That happened more than once.
I'd mix up the days for the welfare center.
I'd call my grandchild by the wrong name.

27
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:38,000
At first I figured — well, I'm just getting old."

28
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Her daughter Soojin noticed something was wrong
the day she got two calls from her mother —

29
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,000
ten minutes apart,
saying the exact same thing.

30
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
[Daughter] "Mom, you just called me."
[Mother] "I did? When?"

31
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,000
[Daughter] "Ten minutes ago."
[Mother] "Oh my, did I? I'm sorry, Soojin.

32
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:02,000
Mom's just so scattered these days."

33
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
After Soojin hung up, she stared at her phone for a long time.

34
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:17,000
An uneasy feeling she couldn't explain —
a cold sinking in her chest.

35
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:22,000
So Soojin took her mother to the hospital.
And there, they received a diagnosis.

36
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:32,000
[Daughter]
"They said early-stage dementia.
That daily life was still fully possible.

37
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:42,000
But that her judgment and memory might gradually fade.
Especially unfamiliar situations, or complicated documents —
she might find those hard to understand."

38
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,000
On the walk out after the diagnosis,
her mother didn't say a word.

39
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:57,000
All the way to the parking lot,
she just walked, looking straight ahead.

40
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:04,000
When Soojin reached for her arm,
her mother said quietly:

41
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:09,000
[Mother]
"I'm fine.
Let's just... walk a little."

42
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
After that day,
the first to reach out was her son Minjun.

43
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:22,000
[Son]
"Mom, I'll come this weekend.
Is there anything you need? I'll get it all."

44
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Minjun lived in Seoul.
He said he ran a business,
though his mother didn't really know what kind.

45
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:37,000
He'd barely come even for holidays, always too busy —
but suddenly he started coming down every weekend.

46
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:48,000
[Mother]
"At first I was glad.
It felt like my son was looking out for me.
His wife came too, and the grandchildren.

47
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:55,000
The house was lively again.
I was just happy."

48
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Every time Minjun came,
he said he needed to organize things.

49
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:12,000
Said his mother had too much clutter,
that as she got older someone should sort things out.

50
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:18,000
And while saying that,
he'd open the document box and look through things.

51
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,000
— Foreshadowing 1 —
One time, she woke from a nap
to find Minjun crouching in front of the document box.

52
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:30,000
He was photographing the documents.

53
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:37,000
When she asked "What are you doing?",
Minjun startled and said:

54
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:45,000
[Son]
"Oh, you're awake, Mom? It's nothing.
I needed some paperwork for tuition.
Just taking pictures."

55
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Her mother let it go.
Surely her own son wouldn't...

56
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:02,000
— Foreshadowing 2 —
The second strange thing was the personal seal.
She'd always kept it in the bottom drawer,
inside an old candy tin.

57
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:08,000
It had been in that same spot for thirty years.

58
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:15,000
But one day, that candy tin disappeared —
and three days later, it was back.

59
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:24,000
She noticed, but with early dementia,
she wondered if she'd moved it and forgotten.
She doubted herself.

60
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:35,000
[Mother]
"I let it go then.
Because I kept forgetting things,
I thought I must have put it somewhere and found it again.

61
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:43,000
That's my biggest regret.
Why didn't I check properly then?"

62
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:54,000
— Foreshadowing 3 —
One day Minjun brought workers to the house.
Said he'd have it fixed up.
Her mother was pleased —

63
00:06:54,500 --> 00:07:03,000
her son was looking after her.
But whether they were really workers,
or whether they were photographing every corner of the house —
she couldn't tell.

64
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:10,000
They might have been real estate agents.
She didn't know that then.

65
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Meanwhile, daughter Soojin was working in Seoul.
She lived alone.
She wanted to come down every weekend
but could manage once or twice a month at most.

66
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:27,000
She called her mother every day.
When she heard her brother was visiting often,
she was relieved.

67
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:40,000
[Daughter]
"I knew my brother was having a hard time financially.
But I thought at least he was good to Mom.
I was too naive."

68
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Have you ever had this experience?
When a family member suddenly starts reaching out often,
and at first you're just grateful.

69
00:07:52,500 --> 00:08:02,000
Neither Soonja nor Soojin knew at the time
what Minjun was preparing.

70
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:18,000
The first strange thing happened
about two months after the diagnosis.

71
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:26,000
On her way home from the welfare center,
Soonja ran into her neighbor, Mrs. Park.

72
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:35,000
[Neighbor]
"Soonja, are you selling the place?
I heard from the real estate office this villa is listed.
Where are you moving?"

73
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Soonja froze on the spot.

74
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:44,000
[Mother] "What do you mean?"

75
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:51,000
[Neighbor]
"Oh, maybe I heard wrong.
I thought someone said Unit 302 was on the market."

76
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
The moment she got home,
Soonja tore through the drawers.
Her hands were trembling.

77
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:06,000
She took out the candy tin.
The seal was inside.
But something looked different.

78
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:13,000
The bottom of the stamp was spotlessly clean —
like new.
An old seal with not a trace of ink.

79
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:25,000
[Mother]
"Did I clean it? Did I?
I can't remember.
But from that day, something felt wrong.

80
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:35,000
My heart was pounding,
but I couldn't put my finger on what exactly."

81
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
She called Minjun.

82
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:46,000
[Mother] "Minjun, are we selling the house?"
[Son] "What are you talking about? Who said that?"

83
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:55,000
[Mother] "The neighbor said it's listed at the real estate office."
[Son]
"Aw, why do old folks make things up like that? Don't worry about it.
Mom, are you taking your medication properly?

84
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:04,000
I heard that in early dementia,
people sometimes hear things that aren't there."

85
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:15,000
After the call, her mother stood holding the phone
for a long time.

86
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:25,000
Was Minjun right?
Was she the one who was confused?
She began to doubt herself.

87
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:35,000
The word "dementia" stuck in her mind.
Maybe she was imagining things.
That thought consumed her.

88
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:47,000
[Mother]
"That was the most terrifying part.
I could feel something was wrong,
but I couldn't tell if it was real or my imagination.

89
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:58,000
The scariest thing for someone with dementia
is losing trust in yourself."

90
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Two weeks later,
Soojin called her mother.
Her mother's voice was different —

91
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:12,000
smaller, without energy.

92
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:20,000
[Daughter] "Mom, is something wrong?"
[Mother] "No, I'm fine."
[Daughter] "You don't sound fine."

93
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:30,000
[Mother]
"...Soojin, when someone has dementia,
should they not handle important things like property documents alone?"

94
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:38,000
[Daughter] "Why suddenly?"
[Mother] "Just wondering. Is that a thing?"
[Daughter] "Mom, something happened, didn't it? Tell me properly."

95
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:48,000
After a moment's hesitation,
her mother told her what the neighbor had said.

96
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:58,000
[Daughter]
"Something felt off.
My brother visiting so often.
Him looking through the document box.
The seal disappearing and reappearing.

97
00:11:58,500 --> 00:12:08,000
The fragments suddenly connected into one picture.
I got chills."

98
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:17,000
That Saturday, Soojin went down to her mother's house.
She opened the document box.
It was empty.

99
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:25,000
Her father's death certificate, the property deed, health insurance documents —
everything was gone.

100
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:34,000
[Daughter] "Mom, where are these documents?"
[Mother] "Documents? Aren't they in there?"
[Daughter] "They're gone."
[Mother] "...They're gone?"

101
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:44,000
The two of them searched the entire apartment.
Nothing.

102
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:53,000
Soojin called her brother.
[Daughter] "Did you take Mom's documents?"
[Son] "What documents?"

103
00:12:53,500 --> 00:13:03,000
[Daughter] "The property deed, Dad's death certificate — all of it is gone."
[Son]
"I don't know anything about that. Mom must have put them somewhere.
She's been forgetting things lately."

104
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:14,000
[Daughter]
"When I heard those words, something inside me hardened.
Whether it was rage or fear, I didn't know.
My hands were shaking."

105
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:27,000
"I couldn't call Mom right away.
I went to the bathroom and washed my face with cold water,
thinking:
If my brother really did this —
what do I do. What do I do."

106
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Do you know what it feels like
to be betrayed by your own family?
The most terrifying moment is when
you accept that it might be true.

107
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Soojin spent that night at her mother's house.
She couldn't sleep.
Staring at the ceiling —

108
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:55,000
at five in the morning, she made a decision.
She would find out for herself.

109
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:13,000
The next morning, Soojin went to the land registry office.
Suwon District Court, Registration Division.

110
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:22,000
She took a number and waited,
praying nothing would come up.
Please let it be a misunderstanding.

111
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:31,000
Please let her brother not be that kind of person.

112
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:43,000
[Daughter]
"The clerk handed me the document.
I knew from the very first line.
My hands trembled so badly I dropped it twice."

113
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Maetan-dong, Suwon — Unit 302.
Owner: Lee Jeonghun.

114
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:56,000
Lee Jeonghun.
A name Soojin had never heard in her life.

115
00:14:56,500 --> 00:15:05,000
She checked the date of the sale contract.
Two months ago.
Right after her mother's dementia diagnosis.

116
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:17,000
[Daughter]
"When I saw the date, my mind went blank.
It was exactly the week after her diagnosis.
The very next week.

117
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:28,000
One week after finding out Mom had dementia —
I just sat down on the bench in the registry office."

118
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Soojin stayed there for an hour.
She didn't even cry.
She just sat there, blank.

119
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:46,000
Then she called her brother.

120
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:52,000
[Daughter] "I'm at the registry office right now."
[Son] "...What?"

121
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:59,000
[Daughter] "I pulled the registration document. You know, right? Who is Lee Jeonghun?"
[Son] "......"

122
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:07,000
[Daughter] "Come down right now. Or I go to the police."
[Son] "...I'll come down. Let's talk in person."

123
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Minjun arrived that afternoon.
At their mother's house.
The three of them sat face to face:
mother, son, daughter.

124
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:30,000
No one spoke first.
Soojin broke the silence.

125
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:35,000
[Daughter] "Did you sell Mom's house?"
[Son] "......"

126
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:40,000
[Daughter] "Minjun. Did you sell Mom's house?"
[Son] "...I did."
[Mother] "......"

127
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:46,000
[Daughter] "Did you get Mom's consent?"
[Son] "......I explained it to her."

128
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:52,000
[Daughter] "You explained it? Mom — did you know?"
[Mother]
"I... I don't know. I think I signed something,
but I didn't know it was selling the house."

129
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:05,000
[Daughter]
"You shoved documents in front of someone with early dementia
and got her to stamp it.
Do you know what that means legally?"

130
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:17,000
[Son]
"Soojin, my debts are overwhelming.
The business completely collapsed.
I asked Mom to lend me money, she said no, so..."

131
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:22,000
[Daughter] "So you sold it without permission?"
[Son] "......"

132
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Then their mother spoke.
Very quietly.

133
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:34,000
[Mother] "Minjun."
[Son] "......"
[Mother] "Those weren't workers, were they."

134
00:17:34,500 --> 00:17:40,000
[Son] "...Mom."
[Mother]
"The people you brought to fix the house.
They were from the real estate office, weren't they?"

135
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:46,000
[Son] "......"
[Mother] "Answer me."
[Son] "...Yes."

136
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:53,000
At that, her mother laughed.
Laughed.
Not tears. Not rage.

137
00:17:53,500 --> 00:18:00,000
Just laughter.
Soojin said that laugh was the most frightening thing of all.

138
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:12,000
[Daughter]
"Watching Mom laugh like that —
it felt like my chest was tearing open.
How utterly bewildered she must have been, to laugh.
How hollow."

139
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:20,000
But then, there was a twist.
As Soojin pressed Minjun further,
he said something:

140
00:18:20,500 --> 00:18:25,000
[Son] "I didn't do this alone."
[Daughter] "What?"

141
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:38,000
[Son]
"Jihyeon... Jihyeon brought it up first.
She said if Mom gets a dementia diagnosis,
her legal capacity might not hold up.
We had to move fast.

142
00:18:38,500 --> 00:18:50,000
Jihyeon prepared the power of attorney form.
Jihyeon set the date for notarization.
I just... followed along."

143
00:18:51,000 --> 00:19:00,000
His wife, Jihyeon.
The daughter-in-law who had always been warm to her.
Who brought side dishes and grandchildren.
Who said "Tell me if there's anything you want, Mother."

144
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:08,000
Jihyeon had planned the whole thing.

145
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:21,000
[Daughter]
"Do you know what Jihyeon did
the day of Mom's dementia diagnosis?
She called a legal affairs office.
That very evening.

146
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:30,000
I later confirmed it from her call records."

147
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:40,000
Hearing that, her mother was silent for a long time.
Then she looked at Minjun and asked:

148
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:50,000
[Mother]
"Even if she suggested it,
you could have said no.
Couldn't you?"

149
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:56,000
[Son] "......"
[Mother] "Answer me, Minjun."

150
00:19:56,500 --> 00:20:08,000
[Son]
"Mom, I was truly backed into a corner.
The interest alone is five hundred a month.
What was I supposed to do?"

151
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:19,000
[Mother]
"And that's why you didn't come to your father's memorial service.
You said you were busy.
Were you busy preparing this?"

152
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:27,000
[Son] "......"
[Mother]
"You don't have to answer.
I understand."

153
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:38,000
In that moment, her mother's face showed a resolve,
Soojin said later.

154
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:50,000
Do you know what the first words were
from a mother betrayed by her own child?
Not rage.
Not curses.

155
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:55,000
Just this one line:

156
00:20:55,500 --> 00:21:02,000
[Mother]
"Soojin.
Find me a lawyer."

157
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:18,000
That Monday, Soojin met with a lawyer.

158
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:28,000
A contract signed by someone with early-stage dementia
could be challenged as void due to impaired decision-making capacity.

159
00:21:28,500 --> 00:21:38,000
Especially a contract signed immediately after the diagnosis —
the chances of winning were high.

160
00:21:38,500 --> 00:21:48,000
And if the power of attorney had been forged,
a criminal complaint could be filed simultaneously.

161
00:21:49,000 --> 00:22:00,000
[Daughter]
"The lawyer looked at our documents and said:
'You can fight this.'
I cried for the first time right there.
In the office, I just sobbed."

162
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:08,000
The lawsuit began.
A suit to void the sale contract.
A criminal complaint against Jihyeon for forgery.
Both at once.

163
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:15,000
At first Minjun held out.
Jihyeon told him not to give in.

164
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:24,000
But when the criminal investigation began,
Jihyeon was the first to crack.

165
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:35,000
Evidence emerged that she had signed their mother's name
during the notarization process.
Her text messages to the legal affairs office were recovered.

166
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:44,000
A handwriting analysis was done on the power of attorney.
It was Jihyeon's handwriting.

167
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:58,000
[Daughter-in-law, from her statement]
"I just thought... Mother doesn't have much use for money anyway.
When dementia progresses, she'll go into a care facility.
I thought paying off the debt was the better choice."

168
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:10,000
[Daughter]
"When Mother progresses, she'll go into a care facility.
That was the mother-in-law she had in her head.
The woman who brought side dishes and held out the grandchildren —
she just saw Mother as a burden."

169
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:18,000
The trial lasted a year.
It was a hard time.

170
00:23:18,500 --> 00:23:26,000
Throughout the trial, their mother lived with Soojin.
At first she was quiet.
She said even food had no taste.
Even sleep left her unrefreshed.

171
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:35,000
But then, little by little, she recovered.

172
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:47,000
[Mother]
"Soojin would walk with me every morning.
Before going to work, one lap around the block.
We didn't say much.
But she was there beside me.
That was the greatest strength."

173
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:55,000
The verdict: their mother's side won.
The sale contract: null and void.

174
00:23:55,500 --> 00:24:04,000
Jihyeon was indicted for document forgery.
The house returned to their mother's name.

175
00:24:04,500 --> 00:24:12,000
The thirty-year home.
The home where her husband's traces remained.
It was hers again.

176
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:24,000
[Daughter]
"The day we received the judgment,
I called my mother.
'Mom, we won. The house is back.'

177
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:33,000
She was silent for a long time.
Then she said:"

178
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:40,000
[Mother]
"Good.
Soojin, thank you.
I think I can go home now."

179
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:50,000
That day, she went back to her Suwon home.
The wind chime was still there by the window.
The one her husband had hung.

180
00:24:50,500 --> 00:24:57,000
With no wind at all, it swayed, just slightly.

181
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:06,000
What happened to Minjun?
Her mother cut off all contact.
She made one phone call to her son.
Just one sentence.

182
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:20,000
[Mother]
"Minjun.
I don't regret giving birth to you.
But I cannot forgive what you did.
I'll be fine, so —
don't contact me again."

183
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:28,000
They say Minjun couldn't say a word.
She hung up.

184
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:38,000
Then she took out the candy tin again.
Put the seal inside.
And placed it back in the bottom drawer —
in the same spot it had been for thirty years.

185
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:52,000
[Mother]
"They said dementia might cloud my judgment.
But this — this was my judgment.
I wasn't clouded.
I made a choice.
And that choice, at least, I made with a clear mind."

186
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:08,000
Today, Soonja left me with one last thing.
After hearing the whole story through,
I asked: 'How are you now?'
And she said:

187
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:23,000
[Mother]
"The most terrifying thing about growing old
isn't getting sick or being alone.
It's losing trust in yourself.

188
00:26:23,500 --> 00:26:35,000
But now I know.
I can still trust myself.
My son taught me that, oddly enough."

189
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:47,000
Dear listeners of Junghee's Night Radio —
how did today's story feel?
It wasn't an easy story.
But Soonja had the courage to share it.

190
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:58,000
These things happen more often than we think,
right around us.
Please check your parents' documents once in a while.
They may not be able to say it themselves.

191
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:10,000
I'm also waiting for your stories.
The things you couldn't say —
Junghee will listen together with you.
Please send your story through the link in the description.

192
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:22,000
Thank you for being with Junghee's Night Radio tonight.
Don't forget to subscribe and set the notification bell.
I'll be back next week.
Good night.

193
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:30,000
[BGM fade out]
